00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:11.930
Rapper 2 Milly, Alfonso
Ribeiro, aka Carlton Banks,

00:00:11.930 --> 00:00:14.880
and The Backpack Kid are
suing the makers of Fortnite,

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:17.080
the world's most popular video game,

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:19.070
for stealing their dance moves.

00:00:19.070 --> 00:00:21.030
You cannot make this up.

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:23.570
But can you copyright a dance?

00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:27.632
In today's Real Law
Review, it's a dance-off.

00:00:27.632 --> 00:00:30.215
(upbeat music)

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:35.530
Hey, Legal Eagles, welcome
back to Real Law Review.

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:38.320
As always, subscribe for
more cutting edge analysis

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:39.940
and stay until the end, when I give you

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:44.270
my prediction for who will
win this ridiculous lawsuit.

00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:46.010
But let's start at the beginning.

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:48.730
Can you even copyright a dance?

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:51.370
Well, the answer, believe
it or not, is yes.

00:00:51.370 --> 00:00:56.310
Although dance is an art form
that is as old as time itself,

00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:58.110
copyright protection for dance,

00:00:58.110 --> 00:01:01.630
specifically choreography,
is a recent development.

00:01:01.630 --> 00:01:03.160
Being able to copyright dances

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:05.240
is a relatively recent development

00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:08.030
which was codified in 1976 when Congress

00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:10.180
changed the Copyright Act to include

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:12.730
choreography and pantomime
to the list of things

00:01:12.730 --> 00:01:14.480
that you could actually copyright.

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:16.350
Congress defined choreography as

00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:17.880
the composition and arrangement

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:20.890
of a related series of
dance moves and patterns

00:01:20.890 --> 00:01:22.950
in an organized, coherent whole.

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:24.300
So, on the question of whether

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:26.580
you can actually copyright dances,

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:28.630
the answer is sometimes, yes.

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:31.770
And the dances in question
here look very similar.

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:34.290
If I had to guess, it looks
like the makers of Fortnite

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:37.340
basically copied 2 Milly's
dance frame for frame.

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:41.210
You can see here that the
dances are almost identical

00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:44.910
and that goes for Alfonso
Ribeiro's Carlton dance as well.

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:47.420
You can see that it is very, very similar

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:50.850
to the dance that you can
buy in the game Fortnite.

00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:53.070
And of course, Backpack
Kid's short dance move

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:55.320
is basically the same here as well.

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:57.690
And there are other dances in Fortnite

00:01:57.690 --> 00:02:00.220
that you can buy as downloadable content

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:03.270
that also look very, very
similar to other artists.

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:06.190
For example, there is the
Poison Dance from Scrubs

00:02:06.190 --> 00:02:08.030
that was created by Donald Faison

00:02:08.030 --> 00:02:11.160
that's basically the same, move
for move in the game itself.

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:12.740
So, it seems pretty clear here

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:15.670
that the makers of Fortnite
are trying to make a profit

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:18.600
off of the dances that were
created by other people.

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:21.160
But just because the
dances are almost identical

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:24.110
does not mean that it's
copyright infringement.

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:27.540
First, a little background
on copyright law in general.

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:28.980
How do you get copyright protection?

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:31.370
Well, you have to have original expression

00:02:31.370 --> 00:02:33.460
that is fixed into a tangible medium.

00:02:33.460 --> 00:02:35.960
Here, video counts as a tangible medium,

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:38.510
so if you record a dance into video,

00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:40.240
that's actually one of the preferred ways

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:42.590
of copyrighting a potential dance,

00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:44.180
so that's not really an issue.

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:46.230
But you should know that copyright

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:48.940
does not apply to ideas themselves.

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:51.300
Only the actual expression.

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:53.410
And you can't copyright functional things,

00:02:53.410 --> 00:02:55.810
that's what patent protection is for.

00:02:55.810 --> 00:02:58.140
And courts have interpreted
the Copyright Clause

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:00.780
of the Constitution as
requiring minimal creativity

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:02.780
in addition to originality.

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:05.860
Originality is the bedrock
principal of copyright

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:08.970
It requires that to qualify
for copyright protection,

00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:10.970
a work must be original to its author,

00:03:10.970 --> 00:03:12.300
and that means that that work must be

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:14.810
independently created by the author itself

00:03:14.810 --> 00:03:16.420
and it must possess at least

00:03:16.420 --> 00:03:19.220
some minimal degree of creativity.

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:21.170
So, for example, you can't copyright

00:03:21.170 --> 00:03:23.720
a single note or a single word,

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:26.780
but you can string a series
of those notes together

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:30.450
to make a copyrightable song
and you can string together

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:34.960
a series of uncopyrightable
words to form an original book.

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:37.240
But there is absolutely
no bright line rule

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:40.170
that says it requires this many words

00:03:40.170 --> 00:03:43.180
to form a copyrightable phrase or a book

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:47.580
or it requires this many notes
to be a copyrightable song.

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:49.630
Certainly, there are very short songs

00:03:49.630 --> 00:03:51.610
and very short literary works

00:03:51.610 --> 00:03:54.070
that despite being very, very small,

00:03:54.070 --> 00:03:56.480
still qualify for copyright expression.

00:03:56.480 --> 00:03:59.790
So, it is an open question
as to how many dance moves

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:02.760
you have to string
together in order to form

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:05.420
a copyrightable dance choreography.

00:04:05.420 --> 00:04:07.110
So, for the purposes of this discussion,

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:09.900
I'm going to assume that these dances

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:12.930
are original to these authors
who are filing this lawsuit,

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:14.300
but it may very well not be the case.

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:16.680
Of course, I am no expert in dance.

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:19.150
Anyone who has ever seen
me dance at a wedding

00:04:19.150 --> 00:04:21.540
knows that that is absolutely the case.

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:23.250
But to me, these are the kinds of dances

00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:25.550
that may very well be
products of popular culture

00:04:25.550 --> 00:04:26.870
and merely popularized by

00:04:26.870 --> 00:04:29.770
the various entertainers
who are filing suit here,

00:04:29.770 --> 00:04:32.120
which would basically doom
their copyright claims,

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:33.770
if they weren't the original author.

00:04:33.770 --> 00:04:35.180
But for the purposes of this video,

00:04:35.180 --> 00:04:38.340
let's assume that they are
original for copyright purposes.

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:40.850
And I will also note that there is footage

00:04:40.850 --> 00:04:43.590
of Alfonso Ribeiro where he claims

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:45.830
that he stole his particular Carlton dance

00:04:45.830 --> 00:04:48.950
from Courtney Cox and combined that with

00:04:48.950 --> 00:04:50.690
some dance moves with Eddie Murphy.

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:51.710
- [Interviewer] How did it originate?

00:04:51.710 --> 00:04:52.790
Was it just like, one of your dances

00:04:52.790 --> 00:04:53.710
that you did when you were younger

00:04:53.710 --> 00:04:55.320
and you're like, "No, I'm bringing it."?

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:57.220
- No, on the Fresh Prince,
they basically had a script

00:04:57.220 --> 00:04:59.640
where it said, you know, "Carlton dances"

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:01.930
and I went, "Alright, well
how would Carlton dance?"

00:05:01.930 --> 00:05:03.737
and I said, "You know
what, I know exactly.

00:05:03.737 --> 00:05:05.470
"I'm gonna steal it."
(others laugh)

00:05:05.470 --> 00:05:07.720
I stole it from Courtney Cox
[Interviewer] Okay.

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:09.130
- and Eddie Murphy.
- [Interviewer] Okay.

00:05:09.130 --> 00:05:10.885
Is it a combination of both of those?

00:05:10.885 --> 00:05:12.543
- It's a combo of all of that
- [Interviewer] Okay.

00:05:12.543 --> 00:05:13.920
- with a little bit of me mixed in.

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:15.350
- [Interviewer] Awesome.
- You know?

00:05:15.350 --> 00:05:17.520
- Now, that is certainly
not the kind of statement

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:19.180
that I would want a client to make

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:21.490
in the context of a copyright suit,

00:05:21.490 --> 00:05:24.130
but it doesn't necessarily doom his claim

00:05:24.130 --> 00:05:27.920
because, again, a song is
always going to be made up

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:31.220
of noncopyrightable
notes to form a melody.

00:05:31.220 --> 00:05:33.610
So, just because there
are constituent parts

00:05:33.610 --> 00:05:35.210
that might not be copyrightable,

00:05:35.210 --> 00:05:37.930
sometimes, and there's a big caveat there,

00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:40.040
sometimes when you add
those things together,

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:42.450
you can create an individual work

00:05:42.450 --> 00:05:43.910
that is original on its own

00:05:43.910 --> 00:05:46.480
or sometimes, they
qualify as a compilation.

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:48.740
But, that being said, it's
not a great statement.

00:05:48.740 --> 00:05:50.910
It just doesn't necessarily doom his case.

00:05:50.910 --> 00:05:52.630
Now, before I dig into the actual lawsuits

00:05:52.630 --> 00:05:55.130
that have been filed and the
dance moves at issue here,

00:05:55.130 --> 00:05:56.320
there's one last thing I need to tell you

00:05:56.320 --> 00:05:59.330
as it regards copyright of
dances and choreography,

00:05:59.330 --> 00:06:03.050
which is that when the House
passed the 1976 Copyright Act,

00:06:03.050 --> 00:06:05.370
it issued a report along
with the act itself

00:06:05.370 --> 00:06:07.130
to clarify some of the
changes that it made.

00:06:07.130 --> 00:06:10.170
And in that House report,
Congress specified

00:06:10.170 --> 00:06:12.880
that choreographic words do not include

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:15.870
social dance steps and simple routines.

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:18.250
Thus, for example,
Congress exempted things

00:06:18.250 --> 00:06:20.980
like the basic waltz
step, the hustle step,

00:06:20.980 --> 00:06:23.430
the second position of classical ballet.

00:06:23.430 --> 00:06:25.800
All of those things are not copyrightable.

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:27.420
But Congress also specified that this

00:06:27.420 --> 00:06:29.800
is not a restriction
against the incorporation

00:06:29.800 --> 00:06:31.990
of social dance steps and simple routines

00:06:31.990 --> 00:06:35.600
into an otherwise larger
choreographic work.

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:37.480
The Second Circuit,
which is one step below

00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:40.640
the Supreme Court, in the
Horgan versus MacMillen case,

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:43.170
held that social dance
steps, folk dance steps,

00:06:43.170 --> 00:06:46.140
and individual ballet
steps may be utilized

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:48.400
as the choreographer's basic material,

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:49.990
much in the same way that words

00:06:49.990 --> 00:06:51.840
are the writer's basic material.

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:54.350
So, at issue here are several lawsuits

00:06:54.350 --> 00:06:56.320
that were filed by the rapper 2 Milly,

00:06:56.320 --> 00:06:58.120
Alfonso Ribeiro, and Backpack Kid.

00:06:58.120 --> 00:07:00.530
They all seem to be represented
by the same law firm

00:07:00.530 --> 00:07:03.090
and they're all filing
suit in the federal court

00:07:03.090 --> 00:07:05.340
in Los Angeles, the Central
District of California,

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:07.620
where I've practiced many, many times.

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:10.350
And in those lawsuits,
the performs are alleging

00:07:10.350 --> 00:07:11.690
that the makers of Fortnite

00:07:11.690 --> 00:07:13.840
and a number of other
video game developers

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:16.630
have basically stolen all
of the different dances

00:07:16.630 --> 00:07:18.750
to include them in downloadable content

00:07:18.750 --> 00:07:20.380
from which they make a profit

00:07:20.380 --> 00:07:23.310
and that that qualifies
as copyright infringement.

00:07:23.310 --> 00:07:26.730
They are fairly bare bones
complaints, but they do seem

00:07:26.730 --> 00:07:29.820
to state the essential elements
for copyright infringement.

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:31.930
So, given that the dances in question here

00:07:31.930 --> 00:07:35.140
are basically identical,
the main legal issue

00:07:35.140 --> 00:07:37.950
is whether the dances are social dances

00:07:37.950 --> 00:07:40.140
or whether they are choreographic works.

00:07:40.140 --> 00:07:42.190
In other words, are these dances

00:07:42.190 --> 00:07:44.350
copyrightable in the first place?

00:07:44.350 --> 00:07:46.610
Now normally, I would
just look up the case law

00:07:46.610 --> 00:07:48.657
and look for cases that
have interpreted the phrase

00:07:48.657 --> 00:07:50.600
"Social dances in the Copyright Act"

00:07:50.600 --> 00:07:52.710
and compare them to cases that have

00:07:52.710 --> 00:07:54.630
interpreted choreographic works

00:07:54.630 --> 00:07:56.690
and give you an easy answer as to

00:07:56.690 --> 00:07:57.990
whether the dances in question

00:07:57.990 --> 00:08:00.780
qualify as social dances or not.

00:08:00.780 --> 00:08:03.950
Unfortunately, there
are very, very few cases

00:08:03.950 --> 00:08:05.300
that have dealt with this language.

00:08:05.300 --> 00:08:07.500
Again, this is a very new aspect

00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:09.490
of the Copyright Law in general

00:08:09.490 --> 00:08:12.660
and really very few people
have even litigated this.

00:08:12.660 --> 00:08:14.870
There's basically one case on point,

00:08:14.870 --> 00:08:15.890
and that, again, comes out of

00:08:15.890 --> 00:08:17.620
the Central District of California,

00:08:17.620 --> 00:08:19.890
which is where a lot of
copyright law originates

00:08:19.890 --> 00:08:21.520
because that's where Hollywood is.

00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:23.810
Basically, the only court case
to deal with this language

00:08:23.810 --> 00:08:26.890
is the court case of Bikram
Yoga versus Evolution Yoga,

00:08:26.890 --> 00:08:30.080
which dealt with a
sequence of 26 yoga poses.

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:33.320
And the issue there was
whether the yoga poses

00:08:33.320 --> 00:08:37.250
would qualify as a copyrightable
work, a choreographic work,

00:08:37.250 --> 00:08:39.750
or whether they were
non-copyrightable in the first place.

00:08:39.750 --> 00:08:41.560
The court sided with the defendants,

00:08:41.560 --> 00:08:44.417
stating that the 26 yoga poses, quote,

00:08:44.417 --> 00:08:46.517
"Hardly seems to fall
within the definition

00:08:46.517 --> 00:08:48.487
"of pantomime or choreographic work

00:08:48.487 --> 00:08:50.657
"because of the simplicity of the sequence

00:08:50.657 --> 00:08:53.860
"and the fact that it is not
a dramatic performance.".

00:08:53.860 --> 00:08:56.600
However, the plaintiff
appealed to the 9th Circuit

00:08:56.600 --> 00:08:58.710
and while the 9th Circuit agreed

00:08:58.710 --> 00:09:00.270
that the defendants should win,

00:09:00.270 --> 00:09:03.450
they did not agree on the
basis for that decision.

00:09:03.450 --> 00:09:05.730
And the 9th Circuit held that
the sequence of yoga moves

00:09:05.730 --> 00:09:07.810
was not copyrightable not because

00:09:07.810 --> 00:09:09.580
it wasn't a choreographic work,

00:09:09.580 --> 00:09:11.920
but because it was a demonstration

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:14.490
of an idea, process, or system

00:09:14.490 --> 00:09:18.370
to which copyright protection
may in no case extend.

00:09:18.370 --> 00:09:20.590
So, given that this is a
matter of first impression,

00:09:20.590 --> 00:09:22.100
where the courts really haven't weighed in

00:09:22.100 --> 00:09:24.330
on what constitutes copyright infringement

00:09:24.330 --> 00:09:26.960
in the context of a
dance or a choreography,

00:09:26.960 --> 00:09:29.290
who's going to win these law suits?

00:09:29.290 --> 00:09:31.498
Well, let's think like a lawyer.

00:09:31.498 --> 00:09:34.331
(hard rock music)

00:09:35.180 --> 00:09:37.530
So, here's what I think both
sides are going to argue.

00:09:37.530 --> 00:09:39.490
First, the plaintiffs are going to argue

00:09:39.490 --> 00:09:42.440
that these dances are very, very unique.

00:09:42.440 --> 00:09:44.060
They're instantly recognizable.

00:09:44.060 --> 00:09:46.030
If you see someone
performing the Carlton dance,

00:09:46.030 --> 00:09:48.030
you know that that is the Carlton dance

00:09:48.030 --> 00:09:50.270
that appeared on The
Fresh Prince of Bel Air.

00:09:50.270 --> 00:09:53.760
And the fact that they are
so unique and so recognizable

00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:55.740
shows that they should
get copyright protection

00:09:55.740 --> 00:09:57.600
despite the fact that
they are very, very short.

00:09:57.600 --> 00:09:59.160
And just in the same way that you can

00:09:59.160 --> 00:10:01.450
string together uncopyrightable notes

00:10:01.450 --> 00:10:03.470
to form a copyrightable song,

00:10:03.470 --> 00:10:06.580
you can string together
uncopyrightable moves,

00:10:06.580 --> 00:10:09.900
movement in the air, to
form a copyrightable dance.

00:10:09.900 --> 00:10:11.977
And it would be weird to make dance

00:10:11.977 --> 00:10:14.720
less copyrightable than its counterparts

00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:16.640
in the literary or musical world.

00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:18.330
I think that the defendants would counter

00:10:18.330 --> 00:10:21.620
by saying that these are not
minimally creative or unique.

00:10:21.620 --> 00:10:23.610
These are just standard dance moves

00:10:23.610 --> 00:10:27.300
that you'll see anywhere, like
a scènes à faire or a trope.

00:10:27.300 --> 00:10:28.480
That they are just putting together

00:10:28.480 --> 00:10:31.200
what people have been doing
for a very, very long time.

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:33.700
And most important, I think
the defendants would argue

00:10:33.700 --> 00:10:36.550
that the dances in
question are social dances

00:10:36.550 --> 00:10:38.350
and therefore, not copyrightable.

00:10:38.350 --> 00:10:41.750
They are far more akin to
the foxtrot or the waltz

00:10:41.750 --> 00:10:45.120
than they are to an
entire hour-long ballet,

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:47.920
a work of choreography, that was intended

00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:50.380
to receive copyright protection.

00:10:50.380 --> 00:10:51.900
In my own personal view, as someone

00:10:51.900 --> 00:10:54.970
who has litigated many,
many copyright issues,

00:10:54.970 --> 00:10:58.000
I think the defendants have
the better argument here.

00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:00.140
To me, if the phrase social dance,

00:11:00.140 --> 00:11:02.260
the exception to the Copyright Clause,

00:11:02.260 --> 00:11:04.180
is meant to have any meaning whatsoever,

00:11:04.180 --> 00:11:08.070
I think it's meant to include
the dances at issue here.

00:11:08.070 --> 00:11:10.230
I think that Congress intended to create

00:11:10.230 --> 00:11:14.270
a differentiation between
a large choreographed work

00:11:14.270 --> 00:11:16.900
and just a social dance like the waltz.

00:11:16.900 --> 00:11:19.130
Maybe the Macarena would qualify as well.

00:11:19.130 --> 00:11:21.220
